       <item row="3" column="0">
        <widget class="QWidget" name="classAttrWidgetGroup" native="true">
         <property name="whatsThis">
          <string>&lt;p&gt;How you configure an actor's stats will depend on whether Auto-Calc Stats is enabled (and it must be enabled if PC Level Mult is enabled).&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;If Auto-Calc Stats is &lt;b&gt;disabled&lt;/b&gt;, then this actor's attributes and skills must be set manually. The base values are determined by the actor's race, but you can apply buffs or nerfs to their attributes, and buffs to their skills. (You cannot nerf skills.) In this case, the actor's class only ends up influencing niche stats like the number of &quot;voice points&quot; they have available for using Shouts.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;On the other hand, if Auto-Calc Stats is &lt;b&gt;enabled&lt;/b&gt;, then this actor's attributes and skills will be calculated automatically based on their race, class, and level. Basically, the actor undergoes similar character progression to player-characters in &lt;i&gt;The Elder Scrolls IV: Oblivion&lt;/i&gt;: they earn a set amount of skill and attribute points per level, and how these are distributed depends on their class. You can tweak the actor slightly by applying offsets to their attributes, but not to their skills.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Note that either way, the game can also apply different health bonuses to the player or NPCs.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr/&gt;
&lt;p&gt;For each attribute, the &quot;offset&quot; is a buff or nerf you can change per actor, the &quot;base&quot; value is determined entirely by the actor's race, and the &quot;calculated&quot; value is the final value of the attribute (i.e. the value with all offsets, class-related gains, and bonuses from Game Settings applied).&lt;/p&gt;</string>
         </property>
