      <layout class="QGridLayout" name="gridLayout_13" rowstretch="0,0,1" columnstretch="2,2,1">
       <item row="0" column="0">
        <widget class="QGroupBox" name="aiPersonalityGroupbox">
         <property name="title">
          <string>Personality</string>
         </property>
         <layout class="QGridLayout" name="gridLayout_10">
          <item row="0" column="0">
           <widget class="QLabel" name="label_36">
            <property name="text">
             <string>Mood:</string>
            </property>
            <property name="buddy">
             <cstring>mood</cstring>
            </property>
           </widget>
          </item>
          <item row="1" column="1">
           <widget class="QSpinBox" name="aiEnergy">
            <property name="whatsThis">
             <string>&lt;p&gt;A value in the range [0, 100]. In past Bethesda titles, it determined how often an actor would move to a new location while executing a &quot;Sandbox&quot; AI package. Its current function is unknown; it may be unused.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Games before Skyrim had a very limited system for defining NPC schedules: there were a fixed set of hardcoded behaviors (ambush, eat, sandbox, etc.), and each AI package consisted of a single behavior and a very, very limited set of options — mostly just on/off toggles. Skyrim expanded the AI system, turning these hardcoded behaviors into &quot;procedures,&quot; allowing a package to use several procedures at a time, and allowing procedures to take more options.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;In past games, the hardcoded &quot;sandbox&quot; behavior checked an actor's energy value. In Skyrim, &quot;Energy&quot; is now an option that can be passed to the &quot;Sandbox&quot; procedure, and there doesn't appear to be a way to tell the procedure to use the actor's energy value.&lt;/p&gt;</string>
            </property>
            <property name="alignment">
             <set>Qt::AlignRight|Qt::AlignTrailing|Qt::AlignVCenter</set>
            </property>
            <property name="maximum">
             <number>100</number>
            </property>
           </widget>
          </item>
          <item row="2" column="1">
           <widget class="DKFormPicker" name="giftFilter">
            <property name="whatsThis">
             <string>&lt;p&gt;This isn't used by the game engine. However, you can access it from a Papyrus script using &lt;code&gt;myActor.GetGiftFilter()&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Skyrim allows Papyrus scripts to open a &quot;gift menu&quot; on an actor, which lets the player give items to the actor. For example, a script could be attached to a &quot;feed your pet&quot; dialogue option to let the player give their pet a food item. Bethesda seems to have originally intended for an actor's Gift Filter to limit what items the player can give them. However, they later modified the script engine so that the script itself chooses what filter to use. This is more flexible: it means that &quot;feed your pet&quot; and &quot;give your pet a toy&quot; can be separate dialogue options, with separate gift filters, on the same actor.&lt;/p&gt;</string>
            </property>
           </widget>
          </item>
          <item row="0" column="1">
           <widget class="QComboBox" name="mood">
            <item>
             <property name="text">
              <string>Neutral</string>
             </property>
            </item>
            <item>
             <property name="text">
              <string>Angry</string>
             </property>
            </item>
            <item>
             <property name="text">
              <string>Afraid</string>
             </property>
            </item>
            <item>
             <property name="text">
              <string>Happy</string>
             </property>
            </item>
            <item>
             <property name="text">
              <string>Sad</string>
             </property>
            </item>
            <item>
             <property name="text">
              <string>Surprised</string>
             </property>
            </item>
            <item>
             <property name="text">
              <string>Puzzled</string>
             </property>
            </item>
            <item>
             <property name="text">
              <string>Disgusted</string>
             </property>
            </item>
           </widget>
          </item>
          <item row="2" column="0">
